context:
The generateStaticParams function can be used in combination with dynamic route segments to statically generate routes at build time instead of on-demand at request time.
```tsx filename="app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx" switcher
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}
```
```jsx filename="app/blog/[slug]/page.js" switcher
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}
```
The primary benefit of the generateStaticParams function is its smart retrieval of data. If content is fetched within the generateStaticParams function using a fetch request, the requests are automatically memoized. This means a fetch request with the same arguments across multiple generateStaticParams, Layouts, and Pages will only be made once, which decreases build times.
Use the migration guide if you are migrating from the pages directory.
See generateStaticParams server function documentation for more information and advanced use cases.

answer:
To utilize `generateStaticParams` within dynamic routes, follow these steps on Next.js 12+ versions using the new API called "Middleware-based routing". This is more efficient than traditional serverless functions and will be slightly different from previous generation methods in newer version of next.js such as v9 to get rid off async/await syntax:
```tsx filename="app/_middleware.ts (Next.JS 13+)" switcher
export function middleware(request) {
    // This is a serverless function that gets executed on every request, allowing you access the dynamic route params using `request` object's query or body parameters: e.g., const slug = request.query['slug'];  OR let jsonBody = await request.json(); and so forth...
    return NextResponse.next() // Or whatever your middleware returns, such as redirecting to another route (e.g.: `return NextResponse.redirect('/some-other-route')`)
}
```
The main benefit of this method is the ability for data fetching within a fetch request inside generateStaticParams function using dynamic routes will be done only once at build time, reducing load times and increasing your application's speed efficiency by caching requests. Note that `generateStaticPath()` (serverless functions) should not use async/await syntax as it does with middleware-based routing in Next.js 13+ versions or higher which is a different approach altogether from the old generateStatics function on next js v9 and older version of this API was used to implement dynamic routes generation at build time using fetch requests for data retrieval within `generateStaticParams` method, not serverless functions as in Next.js 12+ versions where you can use middleware-based routing (async/await) directly on your pages directory files e.g.:
```tsx filename="app/(blog|posts)/[slug].ts" switcher dynamicRouteSwitcher
export async function getStaticPaths() {  // This is for serverless functions or Middleware based rounting in Nextjs v9 and newer versions (async/await) e.g.: return Promise.all([...])   }    const res = await fetch('https://......'); let data= await res.json();
return {paths:data.map(post =>({params :{ slug: post.slug}})) , fallback: false}  //fall back to index page for any other routes not specified in the `getStaticPaths` method (optional) }    const blog = await fetch('https://......').then((res )=> res .json())
return { paths :blog,fallback:'/'} ;   }} })() => Promise.all([...])  // This is an example serverless function for generating static params with dynamic routes in Nextjs v9 and newer versions (async/await) e.g.: return [{params:{} },...] or { paths:[], fallback : false }
```   If you're using a different version of next, your approach may differ but the basic concept remains similar for all modern frameworks like Nextjs v9 and later versions as they use middleware-based routing.  This should help in achieving what dynamic routes have been designed to do on previous iterations within getStaticPaths methods or serverless functions using fetch requests inside generateStaticsParams method, if any difference between the two approaches is clear for you I would be glad too provide an example of how it'll look like with Nextjs v9 and newer versions.

